After install completes, restart the runtime (see section 5).

3. Install PaddlePaddle + PaddleOCR — GPU Build
Use when a T4 or other GPU is attached (see section 4 to check).

python
!pip install paddlepaddle-gpu==3.2.0 -i https://www.paddlepaddle.org.cn/packages/stable/cu126/
!pip install paddleocr==3.7.0
GPU wheel is ~1.9 GB → download takes ~5–15 minutes depending on
Colab's network speed.

The install also pulls CUDA 12.6 libraries (nvidia-cudnn-cu12,
nvidia-cublas-cu12, etc.). This downgrades NVIDIA libraries
that Colab pre-installed for PyTorch, which breaks PyTorch.

⚠️ Known side effect — Torch breaks after installing Paddle GPU
You don't use PyTorch in this project. But PaddleOCR 3.x tries to
import transformers, which tries to import torch. If torch is
broken, PaddleOCR import fails.

Fix: uninstall torch after installing Paddle GPU.

python
!pip uninstall -y torch torchvision torchaudio
Then restart the runtime.

⚠️ Colab GPU quota
Colab's free tier limits GPU usage to a rolling ~12-hour window per
day. Once exhausted, Runtime → Change runtime type → T4 GPU shows
"Cannot connect to GPU backend."

Options:

Wait ~12–24 hours — quota resets on US Pacific calendar day.

Click "Connect without GPU" — proceed with CPU install (section 2).

Use a different Google account.

Upgrade to Colab Pro ($10/mo) — removes daily cap, adds L4 + A100.

Use Kaggle Notebooks — 30 hrs/week of T4/P100, separate quota.

4. Check Whether a GPU Is Attached
Method 1 — nvidia-smi (fastest):

python
!nvidia-smi
If a GPU is attached → prints a table with Tesla T4 (or similar) + memory info.

If no GPU → prints command not found or could not communicate with the NVIDIA driver.

Method 2 — Colab UI:

Menu: Runtime → Change runtime type. The currently selected
hardware accelerator is shown in the dialog.

Method 3 — Paddle's view:

python
import paddle
print("Compiled with CUDA: ", paddle.device.is_compiled_with_cuda())
print("CUDA device count:  ", paddle.device.cuda.device_count() if paddle.device.is_compiled_with_cuda() else 0)
print("Current device:     ", paddle.device.get_device())
Scenario	Compiled	Device count	Current device
GPU attached + GPU wheel	True	1	gpu:0
CPU-only runtime + CPU wheel	False	0	cpu
CPU-only runtime + GPU wheel	True	0	cpu
Rule of thumb:

nvidia-smi succeeds AND device_count() > 0 → GPU is truly available.

If nvidia-smi fails, install the CPU wheel (section 2). Do not
download the 1.9 GB GPU wheel for a CPU-only runtime.

5. What to Do After Every Runtime Restart
Colab resets the filesystem on every runtime restart (or type change).
Drive and installed pip packages have different persistence rules.

Resource	Persists across restart?
Files on Drive (/content/drive/MyDrive/...)	✅ Yes
Files on /content/ (local disk)	❌ No
pip-installed packages (paddle, paddleocr)	❌ No (if runtime type changed)
pip-installed packages (same runtime type)	✅ Yes
Model cache at /content/drive/MyDrive/paddlex_models	✅ Yes
The two-step restart routine
Run the Setup Cell (section 1). This remounts Drive, clones the
repo, sets the model cache path, and tells you whether Paddle is
installed.

If Paddle is missing → run section 2 (CPU) or 3 (GPU), then
restart the runtime and run section 1 again.

Same-runtime-type restarts (no pip reinstall needed)
If you only pressed Runtime → Restart session without changing the
hardware type, pip packages survive. Just run the Setup Cell.

Hardware-type changes (pip reinstall required)
Switching between CPU / T4 / etc. wipes pip packages. You must run
the install cell again.

